Rust Day 8: Tuples & arrays, explained line by line. Group values together, destructure them, index them, and see what Rust does when you go past the end.

📌 In this short:
✅ Tuples mix types, have a fixed length, destructure with let (a, b, c) = tup, and index with .0
✅ Arrays hold one type with a fixed length: [1, 2, 3], [0; 3], a[0], a.len()
✅ Indexing past the end: caught at compile time, or a panic at runtime (never random memory)

💻 Code from the video:
fn main() {
    let tup = (500, 6.4, 'x');
    let (a, b, c) = tup;
    let first = tup.0;
    let arr = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
    let zeros = [0; 3];
    println!("{a} {b} {c} {first} {} {zeros:?}", arr.len());
}

📖 Learn more: https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/ch03-02-data-types.html
